# A string joined onto itself is a builder, and the seed is converted where it
# enters the group from outside, because a builder writes into the header it
# was given. The group's own recursive call is exempt: it already holds one.
# A call arriving from anywhere else re-seeded, and seeding copies the whole
# string — so passing the accumulator through one intermediate function cost a
# copy every iteration. bytes_malloc was 40 for these 40 appends; it is 25 now,
# because seeding is skipped wherever the header still has room and happens
# only when the buffer is full, which is the growth it would have paid anyway.
#
# The same appends without the hop cost one malloc, not 25: written that way
# the loop never reaches the builder path at all. Closing that gap is a
# separate thread; what this pins is that the hop no longer charges per
# iteration.
fn build_onto acc 0
  acc

fn build_onto acc n
  grown = "{acc}xx"
  handed grown n

fn handed grown n
  build_onto grown (n - 1)

pub play = print (build_onto "" 40)
